    shift_txt = (f". Because the alignment of the source's weather fields with the local clock cannot be confirmed (Section {L['sec:data']}), the backtest was repeated with the whole wind series shifted against the load and tariff clock by {', '.join(f'{k:+d}' for k in ks_)} h, each shift defining a reassigned wind clock on which forecasts, calibration, and residuals use only observations preceding the forecast origin ({T('tab:shift')}); these are synthetic alignment experiments, not executable alternatives on the original timestamps. The stochastic plan sheds load on {', '.join(str(Sh[str(k)]['S_cal_shed_days']) for k in ks_)} days against {', '.join(str(Sh[str(k)]['EV_shed_days']) for k in ks_)} for the expected-value plan; the mean gap ranges from {min(Sh[str(k)]['EV_d'] for k in ks_):.2f} to {max(Sh[str(k)]['EV_d'] for k in ks_):.2f} units per day (base {b_['EV_d']:.2f}); the 14 August event recurs under shifts of {', '.join(f'{k:+d}' for k in ks_ if Sh[str(k)]['aug14_EV_gap'] > 1000)} h but not under {', '.join(f'{k:+d}' for k in ks_ if Sh[str(k)]['aug14_EV_gap'] <= 1000)} h, where the gap is smaller and its interval {'includes' if any(Sh[str(k)]['EV_ci'][0] <= 0 for k in ks_) else 'excludes'} zero for at least one alignment. We therefore present the reliability finding as the robust result and the size of the mean saving as alignment-dependent")
