add("# Response to the Editor and Reviewers"); add()
add("**Manuscript EAI-D-26-00883, *Energy and AI***  "); add("*From Wind Forecast Uncertainty to Decision Value: Probabilistic Time-Series Foundation Models for Stochastic Microgrid Scheduling*"); add()
add("Dear Editor,"); add()
add("We thank you and the reviewers for a careful and constructive assessment. The reports converge on a small number of substantive requests — a rolling multi-day backtest scored on realized costs, an ablation isolating the economic contribution of calibration, a locally trained probabilistic comparator, an explicit treatment of the information available to the second-stage dispatch, and the correction of an inconsistency between Tables 4 and 6 — together with textual and formatting improvements. We have addressed every point. Section 1 below explains a correction to the data description that affects every numerical result; Section 2 summarizes the main changes; the point-by-point responses follow. Reviewer comments are condensed in italics. Section, table, and figure numbers refer to the revised manuscript, which has continuous line numbers; a version with all changes marked is provided.")
add()
add("## 1. Correction of the data description and its consequences"); add()
add("In preparing the revision we re-examined the provenance and processing of the input data. The driving data of the study are a publicly available record of hourly weather and electricity-consumption measurements at a single site (the *Smart Home Dataset with Weather Information*, derived from the Smart\\* project), not operational measurements from an agro-export manufacturing microgrid as the original manuscript stated. The original processing also contained two errors that the re-examination exposed: the wind speed, which the source records in miles per hour (its weather fields use US customary units throughout), was used as metres per second; and the demand profile was taken from a separate operational-style file whose load column has no persistence (lag-1 autocorrelation −0.02) and no daily cycle. We have corrected all of this (Table A), stated that the source's time convention and calendar year are undocumented and are therefore assumptions (tested by a clock-alignment sensitivity analysis), narrowed our description of the hourly aggregation to what we could check, described the microgrid as an illustrative configuration specified by the authors (with the same unit, battery, grid, and tariff parameters as before), and recomputed every result. The code, the frozen forecast arrays, and the daily backtest records are released in the repository. We apologize for the error.")
add()
add("| Issue | Original manuscript | Revised manuscript |"); add("|---|---|---|")
add("| Data source | Described as confidential operational data of an agro-export microgrid | Public hourly weather and consumption record, cited; microgrid is an illustrative configuration (Section " + L["sec:data"] + ") |")
add(f"| Wind-speed unit | Raw values used as m/s | Converted from mph (×0.44704); 10 m mean {f(prep['wind_ms_10m_mean'])} m/s, hub-height mean {f(prep['wind_ms_hub_mean'])} m/s |")
add("| Timestamps | Recorded hour used as local time | Source time zone undocumented; interpreted as UTC (supported by the photovoltaic channel, not by the weak diurnal cycle of the weather fields) and shifted to US Eastern time; no year in the source, so the record is placed on an assigned 2023 simulation calendar; one non-existent DST hour interpolated |")
add(f"| Aggregation | Not described | Hourly interval energy (kWh) = sum of one-minute kW readings × 1/60 h; the final conversion step was checked against a January processing trace, the minute-to-hour aggregation is taken from the source processing |")
add(f"| Demand | One 24-h profile of hourly medians from a separate file without daily structure | Consumption series of the same record, affinely rescaled to an industrial load (mean {f(prep['demand_kw_mean'],0)} kW, lag-24 autocorrelation {f(prep['demand_lag24_autocorr'])}) |")
add("| CNN-LSTM versions | Two versions mixed across tables and repository | One frozen forecast array per model and seed (Tables " + L["tab:point_forecast"] + " and " + L["tab:forecast_significance"] + ") |")
add("| Solver | Gurobi | HiGHS (open source); reproduces the original Gurobi objective and commitment on the original inputs exactly |")
add()
add(f"**Consequences.** The forecasting conclusions are unchanged in substance (no statistically significant difference was detected between Chronos-2 and the CNN-LSTM; calibration improves coverage). The decision conclusions change. On the representative day, the very large value of the stochastic solution reported originally (707.53 units, 148.6% of the objective) does not hold: with the corrected inputs the stochastic and expected-value plans select the same commitment and the VSS is zero. That claim is withdrawn. The economic evaluation now rests on the rolling backtest that the reviewers requested: over {nd} observed days, the stochastic plan has a lower mean realized cost and a smaller regret against perfect information than each of the five forecast-based deterministic plans, and it sheds load on {sh['S_cal']['days']} day against {sh['EV']['days']} for the expected-value plan; its cost advantage is concentrated in a few high-demand days. Calibration improves coverage but rarely changes commitments, and when it does it slightly raises the cost. The title (\"Stochastic Microgrid Scheduling\" instead of \"Risk-Aware Microgrid Optimization\"), abstract, research questions, contributions, discussion, and conclusions have been rewritten to match these results.")
add()
add("## 2. Summary of the main changes"); add()
for x in [f"Corrected data description, units, and timestamps; all results recomputed ({S('sec:data')}).",
          f"New rolling day-ahead backtest on {nd} observed days with realized costs, by season and wind regime, with dependence-aware paired statistics ({S('sec:backtest')}; Tables {L['tab:backtest']}–{L['tab:backtest_variants']}; {Fg('fig:backtest')}).",
          f"Calibration ablation (calibrated, uncalibrated, raw-residual scenarios) under identical settings on every backtest day ({S('sec:backtest')}).",
          f"Two locally trained probabilistic comparators (linear quantile regression; CNN-LSTM with residual quantiles) through the identical pipeline; five-seed statistics and training cost ({T('tab:point_forecast')}, {T('tab:training_cost')}, {T('tab:comparators')}, {T('tab:backtest_test')}).",
          f"Information structure stated explicitly ({Fg('fig:timeline')}); hourly rolling-horizon dispatch of every commitment and a decision-timing variant ({T('tab:rolling')}).",
          f"Dependence-aware conformal variants ({T('tab:cal_variants')}), split-sample check, dependence before/after alignment ({T('tab:dependence')}), VSS/EVPI on the unreduced set ({T('tab:vss')}), alternative deterministic benchmarks ({T('tab:benchmarks')}), unit-commitment variants ({T('tab:uc_variants')}, {T('tab:backtest_variants')}), power-curve sensitivity ({T('tab:powercurve')}), rare-event robustness of the backtest — exclusion of the dominant day and repricing or re-optimization under other values of lost load ({T('tab:robust')}) — a clock-alignment sensitivity of the backtest ({T('tab:shift')}), and scenario validation against observed days with a randomized PIT and dependence-aware intervals ({S('sec:scen_val')}, {Fg('fig:validation')}).",
          "Formatting: continuous line numbers, justified text with first-line indent, uniform single-page tables, colour-blind-safe figures with labels of at least 10 pt, a single caption for the three-panel dispatch figure, hedged wording, and the repeated restatements removed."]:
    add("- " + x)
add()
