add("## 4. Reviewer 1"); add()
comment("R1.1", "Frame the novelty as forecast-to-decision integration; avoid language implying novelty in Chronos-2, conformal prediction, SAA, or the unit-commitment model.")
resp("Agreed. The contribution statement now says explicitly that these components are used as published and that the contribution is their integration and decision-centric validation; \"novel\" and \"introduces\" have been removed from the abstract, introduction, and conclusions.", "Section 1.3; abstract; conclusions.")
comment("R1.2", "Only Chronos-2 receives a probabilistic route into the optimizer; add a locally trained probabilistic comparator under the same settings.")
q9, c9, h9 = t9.loc["Quantile regression"], t9.loc["CNN-LSTM residual quantiles"], t9.loc["Chronos-2"]
resp(f"We added two: a direct multi-horizon linear quantile regression trained on the pinball loss, and the CNN-LSTM with residual-quantile intervals. Both are calibrated with the same horizon-wise CQR, generate scenarios with the same residual-trajectory method and seeds, and are reduced and optimized identically. In probabilistic terms the locally trained models are sharper: calibrated interval score {f(q9.cal_IS)} (quantile regression) and {f(c9.cal_IS)} (CNN-LSTM) against {f(h9.cal_IS)} for Chronos-2 ({T('tab:comparators')}), so the foundation model is competitive rather than superior. In decision terms, on the {N['n_test']} test-period days on which the trained models are out of sample, their stochastic plans differ from the Chronos-2 plan by {f(tq['mean_diff'])} and {f(tc['mean_diff'])} units per day (intervals {ci(tq['ci_lo'], tq['ci_hi'])} and {ci(tc['ci_lo'], tc['ci_hi'])}), and no difference is detected for either; the CNN-LSTM-based plan is numerically cheaper ({T('tab:backtest_test')}). We state this as an absence of a detected difference over {N['n_test']} days, not as evidence of equivalence, which would require a pre-specified margin and a longer evaluation period.", f"{S('metho')}.2 (two new paragraphs); {T('tab:comparators')}; {T('tab:backtest_test')}.")
v = t8v.set_index("Variant")
comment("R1.3", "Dependence-aware conformal check: non-overlapping calibration origins, a block scheme, or adaptive conformal inference.")
resp(f"We implemented all three: calibration on non-overlapping origins (one per day), a moving-block bootstrap of the correction with 24-hour blocks, and adaptive conformal inference updated online with the 24-hour delay with which targets become observable. All raise coverage from {pc(v.iloc[0].PICP)}% to between {pc(v.PICP[2:].min())}% and {pc(v.PICP[2:].max())}%, with interval scores within {f(v.IS[1:].max()-v.IS[1:].min(),3)} m/s of each other and of the base method ({T('tab:cal_variants')}). The improvement therefore survives a conservative treatment of temporal dependence. We continue to describe coverage as empirical and state the scope of the guarantee in {S('metho')}.4; in the backtest the corrections are re-estimated daily from a trailing window.", f"{S('metho')}.4; {S('sec:calibration_results')} (\"Dependence-aware checks\"); {T('tab:cal_variants')}.")
comment("R1.4", "The validation split is used for both the conformal correction and the residual library; sensitivity with separate subsets.")
resp(f"We split the validation period chronologically into halves, estimated the correction on one and the residuals on the other, and swapped. The mean scenario energy moves from {rd['rep_day']['scen_energy_mean']:,.0f} kWh to {tsp.loc[0,'scen_energy_mean']:,.0f} and {tsp.loc[1,'scen_energy_mean']:,.0f} kWh (the halves differ in wind regime); the stochastic commitment is unchanged in both; the VSS is {f(tsp.loc[0,'VSS'])} and {f(tsp.loc[1,'VSS'])}. In the backtest both quantities are re-estimated daily from a trailing 30-day window, so the choice is varied implicitly across all days.", f"{S('sec:repday')} (paragraph \"Separate calibration and residual subsets\").")
dac = max(abs(a - b) for a, b in zip(t10["residual library (speed)"]["acf"], t10["scenarios aligned (speed)"]["acf"]))
comment("R1.5", "Per-horizon asymmetric rescaling may distort multivariate structure; show before/after dependence and ramps.")
resp(f"{T('tab:dependence')} compares the residual library with the scenarios before and after alignment, in speed and power. Lag-1 to lag-6 autocorrelations change by at most {f(dac,3)}, so hour-to-hour dependence is preserved. The alignment does widen extreme power ramps (99th percentile {t10['scenarios raw (power)']['ramp_p99']:.0f} to {t10['scenarios aligned (power)']['ramp_p99']:.0f} kW; maximum {t10['scenarios raw (power)']['ramp_max']:.0f} to {t10['scenarios aligned (power)']['ramp_max']:.0f} kW). We report this openly, no longer call the alignment conservative, and measure its decision effect directly by the calibrated-versus-raw-residual comparison in the backtest (mean difference {f(raw['mean_diff'])} units per day, interval {ci(raw['ci_lo'], raw['ci_hi'])}, not significant).", f"{T('tab:dependence')}; {S('sec:powercurve')}; {S('sec:backtest')}.")
comment("R1.6", "K = 100 still has distortion; evaluate the same first-stage policy on the unreduced set and report VSS/EVPI.")
resp(f"{T('tab:vss')} reports RP, EEV, WS, VSS, and EVPI on the K = 100 set and on the unreduced 1,000 scenarios (fixed-policy evaluation of the reduced-set commitments, and the 1,000-scenario model solved directly). VSS is 0 on both; EVPI is {f(t12.loc[0,'EVPI'])} and {f(t12.loc[1,'EVPI'])}; the directly solved 1,000-scenario model selects the same commitment. A forward-selection reduction, which represents the lower tail better, also selects it, and ten independent libraries all select it ({T('tab:reduction')}). This separates decision stability from distributional approximation error; the remaining distortion of the reduced lower tail is reported in {T('tab:reduction')}.", f"{T('tab:reduction')}; {T('tab:vss')}.")
comment("R1.7", "Stress-test the VSS with alternative deterministic benchmarks: the mean wind-power scenario and the median wind-power trajectory.")
resp(f"This suggestion proved central. Both benchmarks, plus the power of the mean wind speed, the Chronos-2 median, persistence, and the point forecasts, are evaluated on the representative day ({T('tab:benchmarks')}) and in the backtest ({T('tab:backtest')}). Because the power curve is strongly convex at this low-wind site, the mean-power and median-power trajectories differ by an order of magnitude in daily energy and fail in opposite directions: the mean-power (expected-value) plan is too optimistic on calm days, the median-type plans too pessimistic on windy days ({T('tab:backtest_groups')}). Over {nd} days the stochastic plan's realized cost is lower by {f(ev['mean_diff'])} units per day than the expected-value plan (interval {ci(ev['ci_lo'], ev['ci_hi'])}), {f(md['mean_diff'])} than the median-power plan ({ci(md['ci_lo'], md['ci_hi'])}), and {f(ms['mean_diff'])} than the plan on the power of the mean speed ({ci(ms['ci_lo'], ms['ci_hi'])}). We reserve the term VSS for the comparison with the mean-power problem and call the others policy gaps, and we state that these mean advantages are concentrated in a few load-shedding days (Reviewer 3, Comment 1).", f"{S('metho')}.7 (\"Stochastic value\"); {T('tab:benchmarks')}; {T('tab:backtest')}; {T('tab:backtest_groups')}; Section {L['sec:discussion']}.3.")
off = t13.iloc[5]
comment("R1.8", "Unit-commitment assumptions (initial commitment with zero output, no minimum up/down times, simplified startup ramping) may inflate the VSS; test a more restrictive formulation.")
vtxt = (f" In the backtest, minimum up/down times of 4 h give a stochastic-versus-expected-value difference of {f(vm['d'])} units per day ({ci(*vm['ci'])}) against {f(ev['mean_diff'])} in the base case, and an initially offline fleet gives {f(vo['d'])} ({ci(*vo['ci'])}) ({T('tab:backtest_variants')})." if vm else "")
resp(f"We tested minimum up/down times of 2 h and 4 h, explicit startup/shutdown ramp limits, units initially online at minimum output with a period-1 ramp link, an initially offline fleet, terminal-SOC variants (free, ≥ E0, = E0), and a restrictive combination ({T('tab:uc_variants')}). On the representative day, minimum up/down times of 2 and 4 h, explicit startup/shutdown ramps, units online at minimum output, the terminal-SOC variants, and the restrictive combination all preserve the commitment; the initially offline fleet changes it (to buying from the grid all day, {f(off.RP)} units), and a free terminal SOC lowers the cost without changing it. The VSS is zero in every variant.{vtxt} {S('metho')}.6.1 now states each assumption, and that each day is an independent scheduling experiment with standardized initial conditions, not a continuous simulation.", f"{S('metho')}.6.1; {T('tab:uc_variants')}; {T('tab:backtest_variants')}.")
cv0 = t16.iloc[0]
comment("R1.9", "Frame the CVaR negative result as evidence that risk aversion is inactive for this microgrid.")
resp(f"Done. {S('sec:cvar')} explains why the CVaR solutions at 0.90 and 0.95 coincide on the reduced set (the highest-cost representative — a calm day — carries {pc(rd['rp_tail_1000']['top_prob_mass_of_highest_cost_rep_K100'])}% of the probability, so both tails fall on one atom), repeats the analysis on 1,000 scenarios, and concludes that risk aversion is inactive for this configuration on the representative day; it is not presented as a general result, and CVaR was not evaluated in the backtest.", S("sec:cvar") + "; conclusions.")
comment("R1.10", "Four suggested references.")
resp("We considered each individually. (1) The review of digital twins in renewable energy systems (Mbasso et al., Energy Strategy Reviews 61, 2025) is cited in the new subsection on practical relevance, where continuous deployment of the pipeline is discussed. (2) For the closed-loop forecast–optimize–control paper for renewable-rich microgrids, we could not locate a bibliographic record under that title in Crossref or Scopus; we would gladly cite it if the full reference can be provided. (3) The broad paper on integrating sustainable energy systems and artificial intelligence was not added, as the introduction already cites specific AI-based energy-management work. (4) The systematic review of hydrogen, biomass, biogas, and photovoltaic hybrid systems was not added, since these technologies are not part of the modeled system. No references are cited as a block.", "Section " + L["sec:discussion"] + ".5.")

add("## 5. Reviewer 2"); add()
comment("R2.1", "Extended summary endorsing the manuscript; five suggested references on decomposition- and attention-based forecasting and multi-battery voltage regulation.")
resp("We thank the reviewer for the careful summary and the positive assessment. We must point out that several figures quoted in the summary (the single-day VSS of 707.53, the 476.13 and 1,183.66 objectives, zero shedding over 1,000 scenarios as evidence of robustness) came from the original analysis, whose inputs were misread (Section 1 of this letter); they have been replaced by the corrected representative-day and backtest results. The reviewer's framing — forecast accuracy, uncertainty quality, and decision value as separate dimensions — is retained and is now supported more directly by the backtest. On the references: the wind-power prediction paper with an improved self-attention mechanism and a bidirectional temporal convolutional network (Energy 322, 2025) and the self-attention multi-model fusion paper for load forecasting in regional integrated energy systems (Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence, 2026) are cited in Section 1.2.1. The two further load-forecasting decomposition papers were not added because the study forecasts wind rather than load and one representative fusion paper is already cited, and the LLM-guided multi-battery voltage-regulation paper was not added because voltage regulation in distribution networks is outside the scope of the unit-commitment model.", "Section 1.2.1.")

add("## 6. Reviewer 3"); add()
comment("R3.1", "Optimization on a single day; the 1,000 and 10,000 trajectories are not independent; conduct rolling forecasting–calibration–scenario–optimization–realized-cost experiments over many test days, by season, wind regime, and horizon.")
leaktxt = (f" An index audit and a future-perturbation test (all observations after the origin replaced by noise, {len(leak)} randomly chosen days) confirm that no decision uses future information; every commitment was unchanged." if leak and all(r["S_same"] and r["EV_same"] for r in leak) else "")
resp(f"We implemented exactly this. For {nd} days ({N['first']} to {N['last']}) the pipeline is run causally — Chronos-2 forecast at local midnight, conformal correction and residual library from the trailing 30 days, 1,000 scenarios reduced to 50, SAA commitment — and each commitment is scored on the observed wind and load. The same is done for expected-value, median-power, mean-speed, Chronos-2-median, persistence, and perfect-information plans, and for the trained forecasters on the test-period days. Results are reported overall, by season, and by wind-energy tercile, with paired sign and Wilcoxon tests and moving-block bootstrap intervals ({T('tab:backtest')}, {T('tab:backtest_groups')}, {Fg('fig:backtest')}).{leaktxt} The results: the stochastic plan has a lower mean realized cost ({f(sc_['mean_cost'])} units per day) and a smaller regret against perfect information ({f(rg['S_cal'])} units per day, against {f(rg['MedP'])}–{f(rg['Pers'])}) than each of the five forecast-based deterministic plans; this ranking is limited to those comparators, since the uncalibrated stochastic variant is {f(-unc['mean_diff'])} units per day cheaper (Comment 2), and it sheds load on {sh['S_cal']['days']} day ({f(sh['S_cal']['kwh'],1)} kWh) against {sh['EV']['days']} days ({sh['EV']['kwh']:,.0f} kWh) for the expected-value plan and {sh['Pers']['days']} ({sh['Pers']['kwh']:,.0f} kWh) for persistence. We report plainly that the mean advantage is concentrated: the two plans agree on {ab['commitment_agreement_with_S_cal']['EV']} of {nd} days, one high-demand day accounts for {pc(cc['top_share'],0)}% of the cumulative advantage over the expected-value plan, the Wilcoxon signed-rank test — which ranks the nonzero daily differences, targets their typical size and sign rather than the mean, and treats days as independent, so we report it as descriptive — does not detect a difference ($p$ {p3(ev['wilcoxon_p'])}), and on days on which neither plan sheds, the expected-value plan is cheaper by {f(-cc['noshed_mean_EV'])} units per day. Mean-cost inference rests on the moving-block bootstrap. Because the lower end of that interval lies close to zero, a new robustness table ({T('tab:robust')}) reports, alongside the full result, the gap excluding {cc['top_date']} ({f(N['robust']['excl_EV']['d'])} units per day, {ci(*N['robust']['excl_EV']['ci'])}), the gap with load shedding repriced at 2.5 and 40 units/kWh ({f(N['robust']['reprice_2.5_EV']['d'])} and {f(N['robust']['reprice_40.0_EV']['d'])}){reopt_txt}. Lead time is examined by committing at noon of the previous day from 36-hour forecasts. Reliability and average cost then move in different directions: the expected-value plan sheds on {noon['EV_noon_shed_days']} days against {noon['S_cal_noon_shed_days']} and the daily differences become consistent (Wilcoxon $p$ {p3(noon['EV_noon_vs_S_cal_noon']['wilcoxon_p'])}), but the mean cost advantage falls to {f(noon['EV_noon_vs_S_cal_noon']['mean_diff'])} units per day ({ci(noon['EV_noon_vs_S_cal_noon']['ci_lo'], noon['EV_noon_vs_S_cal_noon']['ci_hi'])}) from {f(ev['mean_diff'])} at midnight, because the single largest shedding event does not recur; we report the two separately; forecast accuracy by horizon is reported in {S('Results')}.2. We now state that all costs are simulated from observed wind and consumption profiles and author-specified prices, not measured plant costs, and {S('sec:protocol')} describes the 1,000- and 10,000-trajectory evaluations as reduction-fidelity and sampling-stability checks only.", f"{S('sec:backtest')}; {T('tab:backtest')}–{T('tab:backtest_variants').split()[-1]}; {Fg('fig:backtest')}; {S('sec:protocol')}.")
comment("R3.2", "The incremental economic contribution of calibration is not isolated; compare uncalibrated, calibrated, and raw empirical residual scenarios under identical settings.")
resp(f"Added, on all {nd} backtest days with identical forecasts, seeds, scenario counts, and optimizer settings. Calibration improves the hourly coverage of the observed wind by the scenario P10–P90 band from {pc(ab['coverage_hourly']['unc'])}% to {pc(ab['coverage_hourly']['cal'])}%. Calibration rarely changes commitments: calibrated and uncalibrated scenarios give the same commitment on {unc['same_commitment_days']} of {nd} days. On the other {nd-unc['same_commitment_days']} the uncalibrated plan is cheaper, so calibration has a small adverse cost effect of {f(-unc['mean_diff'])} units per day (interval for the difference {ci(unc['ci_lo'], unc['ci_hi'])}, Wilcoxon $p$ {p3(unc['wilcoxon_p'])}). Under these procedures the effect is statistically detectable, but its magnitude is economically small ({pc(-unc['pct_of_base']/100,2)}% of the daily cost, about a hundredth of the gap to the expected-value plan). Raw-residual scenarios differ by {f(raw['mean_diff'])} units per day ({ci(raw['ci_lo'], raw['ci_hi'])}, not significant). We explain why: the commitment of this system turns on whether the scenarios contain calm days, which all variants do, not on the width of the interval around the median; on seven of the eight differing days the calibrated plan left every unit off, because the calibration also widens the upper tail of the wind distribution, and those days turned out calm. RQ2, Contribution 2, and the discussion were rewritten accordingly; we claim no economic contribution of calibration and report its small adverse effect.", f"{S('sec:backtest')} (\"Incremental value of calibration\"); Section {L['sec:discussion']}.2; RQ2.")
comment("R3.3", "Suggested reference doi:10.1007/s00477-026-03206-1.")
resp("Cited in Section 1.2.3 as an example of Monte-Carlo-based interval forecasting around a transformer model.", "Section 1.2.3.")
comment("R3.4", "Is the power curve manufacturer-measured or a cubic approximation? Add an uncertainty analysis with respect to the power curve.")
resp(f"It is a parameterized cubic approximation of the Vestas V90-3.0 MW curve between its published cut-in (4 m/s) and rated (15 m/s) speeds, not the manufacturer's measured curve; the equation, the hub-height power law, and the parameters are now given in {S('sec:powercurve')}. No air-density correction is applied and the manufacturer's tabulated curve was not available to us. {T('tab:powercurve')} reports a sensitivity analysis: no hub-height extrapolation, shear exponents 0.10 and 0.20, rated speed ±10%, a logistic curve, and a 105 m hub. The curve changes the scenario energy by a factor of six, but the stochastic commitment is the same in every case; the VSS becomes positive ({', '.join(f(x,1) for x in pos.VSS)} units) in the three cases that put the most energy into the scenarios." + (f" The shear-0.20 case is also run in the backtest ({T('tab:backtest_variants')}): stochastic-versus-expected-value difference {f(vs['d'])} units per day ({ci(*vs['ci'])})." if vs else ""), f"{S('sec:powercurve')}; {Fg('fig:powerCurve')}; {T('tab:powercurve')}; {T('tab:backtest_variants')}.")
comment("R3.5", "Additional probabilistic benchmarks; multiple training runs, tuning budget, and training cost.")
resp(f"The probabilistic comparators are described under Reviewer 1, Comment 2. CNN-LSTM and PatchTST are now trained with five seeds: test MAE {f(t4.loc['CNN-LSTM','MAE_mean'],3)} ± {f(t4.loc['CNN-LSTM','MAE_sd'],3)} and {f(t4.loc['PatchTST','MAE_mean'],3)} ± {f(t4.loc['PatchTST','MAE_sd'],3)} m/s ({T('tab:point_forecast')}); {T('tab:training_cost')} reports parameters, epochs, and training time. The architectures and hyperparameters are those of the original study, where they were chosen by validation-based tuning whose budget was not recorded; we state this and that no further search was run. Seed 42 was designated in advance for the paired tests.", f"{T('tab:point_forecast')}; {T('tab:training_cost')}; {S('sec:data')}.")
comment("R3.6", "Justify the value of lost load of 10 units/kWh; currency, base year, interpretation.")
resp(f"Costs are in generic monetary units on the scale of the illustrative tariff; no currency or base year applies, because the tariff and cost parameters are author-specified, as now stated. 10 units/kWh equals 53–185 times the hourly purchase price, within the range of published estimates for commercial and industrial customers (Schröder and Kuckshinrichs, 2015). It is varied from 2.5 to 40 units/kWh on the representative day ({T('tab:vss_sensitivity')}) and in the backtest ({T('tab:robust')}), where its role is direct, since the stochastic plan's advantage comes mainly from avoided load shedding.", f"{S('sec:data')}; {T('tab:vss_sensitivity')}.")
comment("R3.7", "Minimum up/down times omitted; initial generation and storage, horizon-end ramping, interday linking; sensitivity to minimum up/down times and terminal SOC.")
resp(f"{S('metho')}.6.1 now specifies the initial commitment and output, the initial stored energy (86.2 kWh), the terminal-SOC rule (at least the initial level), how ramp limits apply at startup and shutdown, and that each day is an independent experiment with standardized initial conditions (no carried-over battery energy, unit status, or minimum-time obligations). Sensitivity results for minimum up/down times of 2 and 4 h, terminal SOC free and equal to the initial level, and initial states are in {T('tab:uc_variants')} and {T('tab:backtest_variants')}. See also Reviewer 1, Comment 8.", f"{S('metho')}.6.1; {T('tab:uc_variants')}; {T('tab:backtest_variants')}.")
comment("R3.8", "Sections 3.5, 3.6, 4, and 5 repeat the same schedule and numbers; streamline.")
resp("The repeated restatements have been removed: each headline number appears once in the results and once in the conclusions; the overlapping discussion subsections were merged; the review and the constraint commentary were shortened.", "Sections 3–5.")
comment("R3.9", "Small fonts; Fig. 9 has three near-identical captions; use a colour-blind-safe palette.")
resp(f"All figures were regenerated with labels of at least 10 pt, the Okabe–Ito colour-blind-safe palette, and secondary encodings (line style, markers, hatching by position) so that no information is carried by hue alone. The dispatch figure is now one three-panel figure with a single caption ({Fg('fig:dispatch')}).", "All figures.")
comment("R3.10", "Statements such as \"remarkably competitive\", \"excellent generalization\", \"eliminates load shedding\", \"demonstrates operational robustness\" go beyond the evidence.")
resp("Removed throughout. We now write \"competitive\" or \"comparable\", \"indicates\" rather than \"demonstrates\", report load shedding as counts of days and kWh, and qualify all conclusions as applying to this illustrative system.", "Throughout.")

add("## 7. Reviewer 4"); add()
comment("R4.1", "Identical expected costs and shedding for persistence, Chronos-2 median, and stochastic optimization in the ablation; controlled ablations needed.")
resp(f"The ablation is now run on observed days and has two parts: calibrated versus uncalibrated versus raw-residual scenarios through the same optimizer (Reviewer 3, Comment 2), and alternative forecasters within the same pipeline (Reviewer 1, Comment 2). Deterministic plans based on mean power, median power, mean speed, the Chronos-2 median, persistence, and the point forecasts are scored alongside them ({T('tab:backtest')}, {T('tab:backtest_test')}).", f"{S('sec:backtest')}; {T('tab:backtest')}; {T('tab:backtest_test')}.")
comment("R4.2", "The out-of-sample evaluation reuses the scenario library; rolling backtests on real operating days are needed.")
resp(f"Added (Reviewer 3, Comment 1). The scenario-based checks are now labelled reduction fidelity and sampling stability.", f"{S('sec:backtest')}; {S('sec:protocol')}.")
comment("R4.3", "The second-stage dispatch sees the full 24-h scenario; clarify the recourse assumption and validate with rolling-horizon dispatch.")
resp(f"We did both. {S('metho')}.6 now has an \"Information structure\" paragraph and a timeline ({Fg('fig:timeline')}) stating that the recourse observes the full daily trajectory and that its costs are a day-ahead planning evaluation with perfect intraday foresight. In the backtest every commitment is also executed by an hourly rolling-horizon controller that knows the current hour's wind (a perfect very-short-term forecast, as stated), uses the latest Chronos-2 median for later hours, carries battery energy and unit outputs forward, keeps the terminal battery condition, and implements only the current hour. This raises the stochastic plan's cost by {f(rh['S_cal']['d'])} units per day and its shedding days from {rh['S_cal']['ffs']} to {rh['S_cal']['rhs']}. The increase is similar for every policy, so the cost ranking is broadly preserved, but the strength of the evidence changes: the expected-value plan remains {f(rh['EV']['gap'])} units per day dearer with an interval excluding zero ({ci(*rh['EV']['ci'])}), whereas the intervals for the median-power, Chronos-2-median, and persistence plans now include zero ({T('tab:rolling')}). Because the controller knows the current hour's realized wind, we describe these results as a conditional evaluation under this controller and its information assumption, not as a universal bound on achievable performance.", f"{S('metho')}.6 (\"Information structure\"); {Fg('fig:timeline')}; {T('tab:rolling')}.")
comment("R4.4", "Scenario validation does not show that persistent low-wind events and joint tails match observations.")
pe_, pc_, pr6 = N["pitr"]["energy"], N["pitr"]["calm"], N["pitr"]["ramp6"]
resp(f"Over the {nd} days we compare the calibrated scenario sets with observed daily wind energy, the longest calm run below cut-in, and the largest 6-hour power ramp ({S('sec:scen_val')}, {Fg('fig:validation')}). All three have probability masses (zero-energy days, integer calm-run lengths, zero ramps), so the deterministic mid-rank PIT is replaced by a randomized ensemble rank, which is uniform when the observation and the 1,000 members are exchangeable — the hypothesis under examination. Because days are dependent, we report the mean and variance of the randomized PIT and the proportions of randomized ranks inside central probability ranges, with moving-block bootstrap intervals, rather than ordinary uniformity p-values. For daily energy the mean PIT is {f(pe_['mean_median'],3)} ({ci(*pe_['ci_mean'],3)}) and its variance {f(pe_['var_median'],3)} ({ci(*pe_['ci_var'],3)}; reference 0.083), and {pc(pe_['cov50_median'])}/{pc(pe_['cov80_median'])}/{pc(pe_['cov90_median'])}% of the randomized ranks fall in the central 50/80/90% ranges. We report separately the coverage of the corresponding predictive intervals in kWh, which differs when ties have positive probability: {pc(val['coverage_central']['50'])}/{pc(val['coverage_central']['80'])}/{pc(val['coverage_central']['90'])}% of days. The ramp results are similar. For calm-run length the PIT variance is {f(pc_['var_median'],3)} ({ci(*pc_['ci_var'],3)}) and only {pc(pc_['cov50_median'])}% of ranks ({ci(*[100*x for x in pc_['ci_cov50']],1)}) fall in the central 50% range, indicating a mild under-dispersion of the scenarios for calm-spell length. We describe these as diagnostics with limited power that indicate broad agreement for daily energy and ramps; accuracy of simulated cost tails is not claimed.", f"{S('sec:scen_val')}; {Fg('fig:validation')}.")
comment("R4.5", "Startup-ramping assumption not reflected in Eqs. (30)–(31); physical consistency of committed units with zero generation.")
resp(f"The text has been reconciled with the equations: ramp limits apply between all consecutive periods, including startup and shutdown transitions, which is feasible because every unit's ramp limit exceeds its minimum output; the incorrect statement that a unit may reach any output in its first hour was removed. The \"committed with zero output\" description was inaccurate: in the base case the first-period output is free within the unit's range. A consistent alternative (online at minimum output with a period-1 ramp link) and explicit startup/shutdown ramp terms are tested and give identical results ({T('tab:uc_variants')}).", f"{S('metho')}.6.1; {T('tab:uc_variants')}.")
comment("R4.6", "Tables 4 and 6 report inconsistent MAE differences; reconcile and recompute the tests.")
resp(f"The inconsistency arose because two CNN-LSTM versions had been mixed; we state this openly. All metrics, bootstrap intervals, and Diebold–Mariano tests are now computed from one frozen forecast array per model and seed; the differences in {T('tab:forecast_significance')} equal the differences of the corresponding seed-42 entries in {T('tab:point_forecast')}, which additionally reports five-seed means and standard deviations. The repository has been updated accordingly.", f"{T('tab:point_forecast')}; {T('tab:forecast_significance')}; repository.")

add("## 8. Reviewer 5"); add()
comment("R5.1", "No line numbers; justified text with a 2-character first-line indent; uniform tables that do not span pages.")
resp("Continuous line numbers, full justification, and a 2-em first-line indent have been added; all tables use one style (booktabs), are scaled to the text width, and are kept on one page.", "Throughout.")
comment("R5.2", "Section 2.2: lack of a non-foundation probabilistic baseline.")
resp("Added (Reviewer 1, Comment 2).", f"{S('metho')}.2; {T('tab:comparators')}; {T('tab:backtest_test')}.")
comment("R5.3", "Section 2.6.15: information available to the recourse; rolling-horizon backtest or reposition as planning evaluation; effect on VSS and reliability.")
resp(f"Both done (Reviewer 4, Comment 3). Under rolling dispatch the cost ranking is broadly preserved and the gap to the expected-value plan remains distinguishable from zero, while the gaps to the median-based plans no longer are; the expected-value plan has {rh['EV']['rhs']} shedding days against {rh['S_cal']['rhs']} ({T('tab:rolling')}). The rolling results are presented as a conditional evaluation under the stated controller.", f"{S('metho')}.6; {T('tab:rolling')}.")
comment("R5.4", "Section 3.1: one target day; specify the date and selection rule; multi-day rolling backtest.")
resp(f"The representative day is 25 October 2023, the first day of the test period, selected by this rule before any result was seen; its observed wind energy lies at the {rd['rep_day']['obs_energy_pct_in_scen']:.0f}th percentile of its scenario distribution. The rolling backtest is described under Reviewer 3, Comment 1.", f"{S('sec:powercurve')} (first paragraph); {S('sec:backtest')}.")
comment("R5.5", "Section 3.2.2: inconsistent MAE differences; the repository uses \"CNN-LSTM V2\".")
resp("Corrected (Reviewer 4, Comment 6). The repository now generates every artifact from the same frozen arrays, and the obsolete files have been removed.", f"{T('tab:point_forecast')}; {T('tab:forecast_significance')}; repository.")
comment("R5.6", "Section 3.5.3: distinguish reduction fidelity, sampling stability, and out-of-sample validation on unseen dates.")
resp(f"Done. {S('sec:protocol')} defines the three; reduction fidelity and sampling stability are reported in {T('tab:reduction')}; out-of-sample decision quality is inferred only from the backtest on observed days.", f"{S('sec:protocol')}; {T('tab:reduction')}; {S('sec:backtest')}.")

